WEBVTT

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:27.440
We're back at the very edge of our isles.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.600
But now we're on a whole new kind of adventure.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.400
A unique great guide to our coast.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:40.840
But this is a guide beyond anything you'll find in your average tourist brochure.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:48.800
A guide crammed with local knowledge, amazing discoveries and stunning, secret spots.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:56.640
Coast and her expert crew have spent over ten years navigating this ever-changing natural wonder.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:08.120
And now we're bringing it all together, and more, to give you the ultimate guide to our coast.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.040
We've selected eight stretches of British coast.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.200
North, South, East, West.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.760
And some of the best bits in between.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.320
Each week we'll be taking to the sea in a remarkable array of boats and ships.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.680
We'll have a completely fresh perspective on the coast.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.080
We'll seek out charismatic characters...

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.600
Andy, fancy seeing you here!

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.280
..Momentous events...

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.920
This is Britain's most deadly shoreline.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.120
..Secret spots, and surprising stories.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:54.040
There's no denying that there is a charge to be had from holding something like this.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:02:03.000
A brand-new view of our coast, with all the inside info you need to enjoy these shorelines like a local.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.320
Haul away, sailors, haul away.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.560
This time I'm heading for Wales.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:19.160
This is Coast.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.640
The Great Guide.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.000
Stunning southern Wales.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.800
A wild delight.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:59.720
Towering cliffs, beckoning beaches and rolling surf.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.680
Perfect for extreme sports.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.960
A coastline book-ended by contrasting cities.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:15.680
A capital on the sea, Cardiff and its marina, and, just over 100 miles away, St Davids is Britain's smallest city.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.480
Though, of course, there is a cathedral.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:26.040
The Coast experts have teased out the shoreline's secrets.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.760
That's unbelievable.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.720
I can't believe anybody managed to build that.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.560
Digging deep into every aspect of its culture, nature and history.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:42.400
Our new mission brings you the very best of those breathtaking discoveries, as we get to grips with what gives this coast its wild appeal.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.480
This is our great guide to southern Wales.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:52.400
The new journey I'll be making around this coast is a beauty.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:58.240
Britain's first Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, to be precise.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.880
The Gower Peninsula.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:08.720
Harnessing the wind, I'll sail around its south coast, making for the golden curve of Oxwich Bay.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:16.200
From then on, it'll all be down to muscle power as I kayak to Port Eynon, and journey's end will be Penclawdd on the Burry Estuary.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:29.160
En route I'll be compiling our great Welsh guide from a wider canvas of stories that stretches all the way from Cardiff in the east to St Davids on the furthest tip of western Wales.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.080
My own journey starts at the gateway to Gower - Mumbles.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:43.320
Two outstanding offshore features supposedly give Mumbles its unusual name.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:50.800
The twin islands over there were christened by French sailors Marmelles, which is French for breasts.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.480
Marmelles became Mumbles.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.200
No comment.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:10.560
I'm here to find out if I've got what it takes to master a must-do watersport in our great guide and to succeed I'm going to have to get to grips with a boat that's Celtic to its core.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.240
It's action stations time.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:17.440
I'm casting off in a traditional craft that is making a bit of a comeback with the local community.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.840
Hi, guys.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:27.400
The Mumbles Amateur Rowing Club is going to put me through my paces in a Celtic longboat.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.320
I've never seen anything like it.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.400
Where does it originate from?

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.080
Well, this is a traditional kind of boat design.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.600
It's actually based on the old Irish currach.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:35.960
But there are lots of different kinds of longboats.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:53.200
Sea rowing competitions have taken place in south-west Wales for centuries but the sport was revolutionised 40 years ago when Pembrokeshire boat builder Des Harris crafted a longboat that could cope with the steep waves of this wild coast.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:06:00.520
It became the template for countless copies, known as Pembrokeshire longboats.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:06.440
And today Wales boasts 29 sea rowing clubs.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.640
I used to row, I mean, reasonably seriously.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.240
But it looked nothing like that.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.680
It was like a sort of fibreglass pencil.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:17.880
Oh, yeah, that is the sort of river boats, they are much more about finesse and technique.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.400
This is quite a tubby kind of boat, really.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.040
And it's quite a rough art, really.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.800
You're out in the sea against the waves.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.960
I think it's wonderful.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:28.600
But I think river rowers think it's a bit of a rough old sport.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.000
But we love it.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.200
Each to their own.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.080
I feel a little bit scared getting in with you rough experts.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.520
Sea dogs.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:33.720
Yeah.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.640
So where have you put me?

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.000
I'm stroke side, by the way.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.720
You're going to be rowing in number three.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.120
Number three, OK, fine.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.440
So let's go.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:52.320
This is unlike any boat I've rowed before so I need to pick up a few tips and fast.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.920
Lean back more.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.320
Yeah.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.480
That is better.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:03.240
It's almost like ten to and ten past the hour.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.840
If we leant like this we'd have got kicked out of the boat.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.160
I still feel I'm not leaning forward enough.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.720
No, that's good.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.880
Better, definitely.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.560
My oar's going a bit deep.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:13.720
Lean.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.600
I'm deep.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.560
Two.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.440
After Meg's masterclass, it's time for a challenge.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:29.840
Can I unleash my inner Celt in a race against an experienced longboat crew?

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:36.080
These are hardened rowers whose idea of a good time is a jaunt across the Irish Sea, 90 miles, in one of these.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.720
Three, two, one!

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.240
Row!

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:49.160
I'm forgetting to feather.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:49.560
Sorry, I forgot to feather.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.600
Are we winning?

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.200
Yes, we're winning!

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.760
Bang.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:00.720
Bang.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.720
Oh!

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.200
Are we winning significantly?

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.640
Keep going!

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:24.120
When can we wind down?

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.400
Easy oars, easy oars.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:30.920
That was what's called a decisive win in rowing.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.720
Two lengths?

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.280
Yeah, definitely.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:36.760
Almost rude, but what can you do?

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:45.120
Longboat rowing is only one of the watersports that draw thousands of visitors to Wales each year.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:53.560
Atlantic surf makes these shores a mecca for world-class canoeists and surfers.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.040
But it's not all about the waves.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:03.640
The wide, lonely expanse of Pembrokeshire's Freshwater West beach whips up the ultimate challenge for kite surfers.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:11.120
World champion Kirsty Jones gave us a kite surfing masterclass for our great guide.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.760
It's my favourite beach to come surfing, really.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.840
It's a world-class surfing break.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.000
OK, here we go.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.200
And we're going to hit the wave on this one.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:34.440
Kite surfing is using a big power kite to pull you along on the water.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.920
And you can do tricks, you can do jumps.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.400
Going to do a little grab now.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.240
You can just cruise along on the water.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.520
It's just an amazing sport.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.040
I'm going to go for a forward loop now.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.880
Freshwater West is just amazing when it's like this.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:16.960
Wind power is the best way to appreciate the outstanding beauty of this coast.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.400
I'm leaving the calm waters of Swansea Bay and setting sail around the Gower Peninsula.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:27.480
I'm headed for Oxwich Bay on a boat that's a piece of living history.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:37.400
Built in 1909, the Olga plied her trade around the Bristol Channel for decades, taking out pilots to guide ships into port.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:43.360
Time was money, so the Olga was built for speed but in rough seas that can make for a choppy ride.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:52.280
I've got to be honest, I'm yet to fully find my sea legs.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:58.360
But surely there's no better companion on our great coastal guide.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.640
And no better way to get up close and personal with these waters.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:11.080
Atlantic currents collide with the Bristol Channel, washing in waters abundant with nutrients.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.520
Rich pickings for dolphins.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:18.800
And Wales is one of the best places in Europe to spot them, if you know where to look.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.320
Miranda Krestovnikoff went out with a local.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:30.640
Today we're searching for short-beaked common dolphins, who come here in spring and summer.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.600
Any sign of anything yet?

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.720
No, nothing as yet.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:33.480
We're still looking.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:39.480
Dolphin!

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:50.440
Suddenly, in seconds, we're surrounded by about 20 dolphins, checking us out and riding the bow wave.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:52.960
Oh, brilliant, brilliant, brilliant.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.680
There's one just on the bow and another one.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:57.600
This is fantastic.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.640
So we can see them swimming, we can see them moving, we can see them interacting.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:11.680
Why do dolphins come here?

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.440
What's so special about the waters here?

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:21.480
It's an incredibly rich area for food and like all breeding animals they need the food.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.120
And if the food's there then they're going to thrive.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:30.960
Over the years, we're coming to the conclusion that this is a nursery area.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.640
So, yeah, important.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.000
Probably important in world terms.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:46.360
Usually out dolphin watching you're lucky to get five or ten minutes with them but this group were brilliant and they stayed with us for nearly an hour.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:55.680
But with each encounter, it's becoming clearer that these waters are crucial for families of dolphins raising their young.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.400
And it's a real privilege to watch them do it.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:08.120
The winds off Gower can turn a sedate sail into more of a roller-coaster.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:18.720
But whatever the weather, this untamed peninsula takes pride of place in our great guide, as Britain's first-ever Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.560
National Trust ranger Kathryn Thomas has the local knowledge.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.000
There can't be any more wild way of experiencing the Gower than this.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:34.280
But you can just about make out through cliffs behind us now, coming into view, one of the Gower's sort of iconic beaches.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.640
Let me get this right, it's an AONB.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:39.880
That's right, Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.560
And Gower was the very first one in the UK.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:46.960
So we're celebrating our 60th anniversary this year.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:14:01.640
On a clear day it's a microcosm of contrasting landscapes... ..squeezed into a pocket of land no more than 70 square miles in size.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:09.280
So we've got the dunes and the cliffs and the marsh and the woodland and our famous Gower beaches.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:15.360
We're a bit like the sort of little brother of the National Parks, basically.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.360
There are restrictions on planning, things like that.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:28.120
There is a very timeless aspect, and especially looking onto it from here because you see no development, no cars, not even a telegraph pole.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.640
Yeah, that's what it's all about, it's keeping it like that.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:38.520
We have visitors that come to Gower and they came as a child and they're 80 years old now and they come and they say, "It's not changed."

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:43.440
And looking out today, it's a very uncompromising landscape, isn't it?

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:49.640
You know, the kind of dreich clouds sitting right down on top of the cliff.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.120
Yeah.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:50.800
It's unforgiving.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.840
It is, although...

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.400
Well, we have picked a special day today!

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:59.320
It's interesting to see it from this perspective, though.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:04.560
There's just some slightly terrifying looking caves.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:09.960
This is Coast's great guide to southern Wales.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:15.600
On my journey around the Gower Peninsula, I'm heading for the tranquillity of Oxwich Bay.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:30.480
It's there that I'm meant to spend a night under canvas, hop out feeling fresh as a daisy and plop myself into a kayak, from which, I'm told, I'm going to get the best views of a Welsh stunner, Worm's Head.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.640
This is a coast of true contrasts.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:37.240
Millions are drawn to its natural beauty.

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:43.880
What they may not know is that it nestles next to some pretty heavy industry.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:47.040
Communities here have been created by a hunger for natural resources.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:55.560
Copper, steel, natural gas - they've all seen fortunes ebb and flow.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:16:00.520
The South Wales coast is rich in minerals and fossil fuels.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:09.640
Its geology has shaped its history, from the coal seams of Glamorganshire to the natural harbours of Pembrokeshire.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:18.200
We went time travelling for our great guide, beginning in the 19th century, when Wales was the powerhouse of the Industrial Revolution.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.400
What helped put it there was copper.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:25.800
Swansea once produced two thirds of the entire world's supply.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:31.920
Mark Horton discovered how this city's copper forged one of the greatest victories in British maritime history.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:37.960
200 years ago, Swansea's copper was in demand.

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:38.600
Who was after it?

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.440
The Royal Navy.

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:43.440
Hello, David.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:44.680
Hello, Mark.

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:46.240
Oh, fantastic.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:52.880
David Jenkins knows the story of the city's copper-bottomed deal with Nelson's Navy.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:56.880
This is what gave Nelson's Navy massive tactical advantages.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:00.640
It's a sheet of copper ore from the hull of HMS Victory.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:05.760
You can see here Vivian & Sons, Swansea.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:08.319
Look, and a number, 2802.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:08.839
That's right, yes.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:16.160
Copper ore and obviously copper itself, too, was very, very valuable but its value was not so much monetary as tactical.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:23.800
Indeed, the manoeuvring that took place before the very Battle Of Trafalgar owed a great deal of its success to the fact that Nelson's ships had this on their bottoms.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:33.720
And it means that no weeds grow on the hull of your ship, the water slips much more quickly over the hull of the ship and therefore it gives the ship excellent manoeuvrability.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:39.000
Swansea's dominance of the world copper trade meant that the Royal Navy had copper-bottomed boats but the French didn't.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.920
Swansea was nicknamed Copperopolis.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:03.080
Further along these waters, another industrial powerhouse found fame to rival Swansea in the 20th century.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:11.200
Its very name, Port Talbot, a clue to how crucial the sea was to its existence.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:24.160
Built on the coast, where iron ore could be easily unloaded, a Welsh engineering marvel sprung up - the Port Talbot steelworks.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:26.560
That, too, goes into our great guide.

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:30.200
Four years ago, I investigated.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:37.160
So how do you build a steelworks on something as soft and as shifting as this?

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:43.800
Engineer David French is going to let me in on the secret, using bricks and sticks.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:47.040
So how do you get around this problem of building on soft sand?

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.200
Well, what we need are deep foundations called piles.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:59.720
What we're doing is pushing the pile through the sand, through the thick layer of peat, down into this secure founding stratum.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.520
This is the clay, sticky bit?

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.640
Yeah, this would be a mixture of stiff clay, gravels and sand.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.080
And that's going to hold the still in place.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.720
That's it, you've got it.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:10.160
I see.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:14.000
So can we replicate what was once done here at Port Talbot?

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:14.240
Well, hopefully we can, Tess, yeah.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:14.440
Let's try.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:17.360
HE GROANS I think I'm hitting some sticky clay.

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:20.360
Yeah, right, now hopefully we've got our stilts in and we can put our building on top.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:30.240
I do, yes.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.400
And another one.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:32.080
So who says you can't build on sand?

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:34.440
Yes, it can be done.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:39.040
How many of these piles were driven into the site here at Port Talbot?

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:45.160
Well, amazingly, 33,000 of these piles were installed across the site.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:55.680
Work on the steel plant began in 1947, part of rebuilding Britain after the war.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:20:06.280
Boys have been forged into men here since the early 1950s when steel first rolled out over the sand.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.000
The steady stream of resources flowed into the plant.

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:17.640
With cargo carriers getting bigger, in the mid-1960s a new deepwater harbour began construction.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:29.320
The days when Port Talbot was powered by Welsh coal are long gone.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:38.360
This was once the biggest steelworks in Europe... ..and Wales's largest employer.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.280
It now faces an uncertain future in an unpredictable global market.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:48.720
The sun has set on the golden age of Welsh industry.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:21:00.920
Today, a very different fuel flows in from 7,000 miles away, shipped into a brand-new terminal at Milford Haven.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.320
This energy success story goes into our guide.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:08.520
Nick Crane was there right at the start to witness the birth of an industrial heavyweight.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:15.640
Here in Milford Haven they'll soon be importing natural gas by ship.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:27.200
To a geographer like me, this is a ria, a flooded valley, but to shipping it's one of the deepest and finest natural harbours in the world.

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:37.920
By the end of 2007, some of the world's biggest ships should be navigating their way to this pier, coming all the way from Qatar on the Arabian Gulf.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:46.520
Transporting Qatar's gas 7,000 miles to us relies on a remarkable idea.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.720
Turn the gas into liquid.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:54.080
This refrigeration plant concentrates the gas down into liquid by supercooling it.

00:21:53.120 --> 00:22:05.000
14 supertankers are being specially built to keep the natural gas insulated so that it stays liquid on its trip from Qatar.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:14.920
When natural gas is super-chilled, its volume shrinks by 600 times and this makes it economic to ship.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:21.800
So our gas will arrive here in Milford Haven as super-cold liquid.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:24.440
It's then got to be kept chilled to store it as a liquid.

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:32.720
From the bottom of the site, these tanks didn't look much but up close they are absolutely massive.

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:43.600
Each storage tank in the Pembrokeshire National Park is big enough to contain the Albert Hall, and there are five of them.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:51.840
These tanks act like giant Thermos flasks.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:23:00.880
They'll be full of liquid natural gas stored at minus 160 Celsius.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:08.920
When it's warmed up again it will expand 600 times, making huge amounts of gas ready for us to use.

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:13.800
Today, South Hook gas terminal is in full flow.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:28.560
Over 500 of the world's biggest liquid natural gas tankers have docked here with a helping hand from the astonishing geography of the Milford Haven waterway.

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:38.320
The flooding of this Ice Age valley 11,000 years ago created the deep estuary that today means this terminal can supply up to a fifth of the UK's natural gas needs.

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:47.280
Where copper and steel once flowed out of South Wales to the world, gas now flows in.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:54.280
As the tide of history turns, this coast remains a vital gateway connecting Britain to the rest of the world.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:05.800
On my journey around this southern Welsh shoreline I've reached Oxwich Bay.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:12.000
From there I'll head for Port Eynon beach and secrets hidden in the sands.

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:21.640
I'm hoping for a close encounter with seals at Worm's Head before trying my hand at an ancient art on the Burry Estuary.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:26.400
For now, the weather has somewhat dampened my enthusiasm for camping.

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:35.760
People say the Gower Peninsula has its own microclimate and I certainly feel I've experienced something of that in the last 24 hours.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:43.080
I really wanted to show you some of the cracking views from the beaches here but I'm afraid no can do this evening.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:52.160
Mind you, this unpredictable Welsh weather hasn't stopped generations of day-trippers reaching for their buckets and spades when summer rolls around.

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:55.280
There's some great old footage here.

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:57.560
Even a cheeky holiday snog.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:07.080
A real reminder, in fact, that the beaches here in southern Wales have long been the go-to destination for miners and their families really in need of a holiday.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:19.880
And when it comes to traditional Welsh seaside resorts, there can only be one choice for our guide - Barry Island.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.480
In its heyday, over 100,000 visitors would come to this beach on a bank holiday.

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:27.760
Neil Oliver discovered why.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:38.440
The annual trips organised by the pits and by the Sunday schools of chapel and church were THE highlight of the year.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:45.760
Over 50 years ago, Jane Ward didn't come to the beach alone, her whole village came, too.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.080
Hello, Jane.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:46.480
Hi, Neil.

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:48.520
How are you?

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:48.680
I'm well.

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:49.120
Good.

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:49.600
What's with the giant numbers on the wall?

00:25:48.680 --> 00:26:01.160
Well, when we used to come on trips, people arranged to meet on the beach and we would say we were arriving at different times, we would meet at a certain number.

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:03.120
So, is either of these two children you?

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.160
Yes, here.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:04.360
Fantastic.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:04.920
Brilliant.

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:07.320
How did you get here from the valleys?

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:09.680
By train, steam train.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:17.080
So imagine now, we were 250 Sunday schoolchildren, a mass exodus from the village.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:22.760
So you travelled together and you colonised one patch of beach and you all stayed together the whole day?

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:23.320
More or less.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:29.800
And in the course of the afternoon, we'd start singing our choruses from Sunday school.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:34.240
One would start and then another group would join in and before long you'd have the whole beach singing.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:44.080
After a day of sea, sand and community singing, they were ready for all the fun of the fair.

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:53.880
It's no job for a grown man.

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:11.040
Today, Barrybados, as the locals call it, is enjoying a 21st-century renaissance.

00:27:10.160 --> 00:27:26.400
Barry Island is now the only beach in Wales that offers free Wi-Fi and the Gavin And Stacey effect is helping pull in over 350,000 tourists, all searching for a spot of Welsh sunshine.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.440
Good luck with that.

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:46.440
This is Coast's great guide to southern Wales.

00:27:45.680 --> 00:28:00.440
Our expert team has scoured the Welsh shore for over ten years in search of hidden stories, but if you were on a whistle-stop tour, what are the unmissable sights you must see to say you've experienced this coast?

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:07.840
This is our flying visit to southern Wales.

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:20.600
Many Welsh adventures begin here, with a bridge or two across the Bristol Channel.

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:28.120
Within half an hour, the familiar landmarks of Cardiff and its historic bay draw into view.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:38.320
Just down the coast is St Donat's, a medieval castle rebuilt by the real-life Citizen Kane...

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:42.760
..William Randolph Hearst.

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:49.360
In truth, Hearst wasn't just a lover of history, he was a lover, a man with a mistress.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:58.200
So, a little Welsh hideaway a few thousand miles from home suddenly starts to make sense.

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:02.440
The Kenfig dune system is here.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:11.400
Buried beneath it is an entire medieval village and castle swallowed by the shifting sands.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:16.240
Then, of course, there is Gower's stunning shoreline, right where I am now.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:26.760
The limestone cliffs of Culver Hole hide a medieval dovecote that Nick swung by to see.

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:27.200
As front doors go, this is fairly inaccessible.

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:30.760
I've never seen anything quite like it.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:36.280
And when it comes to beaches, Wales has got some of the finest on the planet.

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:45.520
Rhossili beach on Gower's western edge is Britain's best and in the world's top ten.

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:54.680
There's even a little bit of England in this bit of Wales, Tenby, for those who like their tourism genteel.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:30:03.560
Further on, the coast twists and turns as majestic cliffs and sandy coves dominate the coastline.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:12.200
Hidden in a cleft of these cliffs is an historical treasure, the unique house of worship that is St Govan's chapel.

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:22.720
By now, we're firmly in the Wild West of Wales, St Brides Bay and the island paradise of Skomer, a treat for twitchers.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:34.040
And at the far end of the bay, the most westerly point in mainland Wales, a spiritual haven, St Davids and its magnificent cathedral.

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.440
But if you only do the unmissable sights, then you're missing so much more.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:42.360
Follow us for the fuller picture.

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:52.840
On my journey around Gower, I've reached the shimmering waters of Oxwich Bay.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:31:00.320
I'm preparing to set off in a back-to-basics boat, the best way to get up close to nature on this peninsula.

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:03.520
I'm about to go kayaking and what a difference a day makes.

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:06.680
It's almost flat out there, but, even so, I'm taking no chances.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:17.480
I think I've got on a wet suit, a dry suit and then, somewhere in the middle, a onesie because once I get out around the headland, I've got no idea what's going to greet me.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:25.840
With rocky beauty comes real danger.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:34.120
The coast of southern Wales is littered with the legacy of shipwrecks.

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:35.360
Lighthouses.

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:40.160
We're putting one of these into our guide.

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:47.480
One that keeps a dark and bloody secret, a grisly tale of death and insanity.

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:48.760
It's a secret that would revolutionise lighthouse-keeping on these shores.

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:57.880
Neil flew out to Smalls Lighthouse to discover why.

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:10.880
Back in 1775, a guy called Henry Whiteside won the contract to build the first lighthouse out here and the solution he came up with almost beggars belief.

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:19.120
What he did was, he erected a circle of massive oak beams, great posts rising 70-odd feet into the sky.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:19.520
This is a picture of the thing.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:37.520
People must have thought he was off his head when he showed them this drawing, but his solution was that the waves would break through these massive posts and, instead of breaking them, would just carry on and he was right because this thing stood up to the westerlies for over 70 years.

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:40.400
Now that was surely enough to get this place its spot in the history books.

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:42.680
But there is another story.

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:49.640
The winter of 1800-1801 was a particularly savage one.

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:58.360
And during that time, Smalls Lighthouse was being manned, as was the custom, by a two-man team.

00:32:57.400 --> 00:33:09.000
Now, Thomas Howell and Thomas Griffiths were notorious for one thing and one thing only and that was their constant arguing.

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:17.520
So, when Griffiths died out here on the lighthouse in a freak accident, Howell had a predicament.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:29.640
What he wanted to do, quite naturally, was to get rid of the body but he was panic-stricken in case people would think the pair had had another fight, he'd killed Griffiths and dumped the body to get rid of the evidence.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:35.200
So, in fear of a murder charge, he decides to hold on to the body.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:44.840
He puts it in a makeshift coffin and settles down to wait for the rescue party, but the storm is so relentless that nobody ever comes.

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:56.240
After a few days, the body starts to smell and in desperation he drags the coffin out onto the balcony that surrounds the lamp house.

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:02.480
During one of the worst storms yet, a massive wave shatters Griffiths' coffin.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:18.400
The putrefying corpse spills out and, by a cruel fluke of the way it lands, the wind is able to take one of the arms and blow it thus so that it looks as though the corpse is beckoning people onto Smalls Lighthouse once again.

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:29.120
And this is the sight that greets the rescue party when, after more than three months, the storm has abated enough for them to get out to the lighthouse.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:36.280
Inside the lighthouse they found Thomas Howell, and folk that knew him didn't even recognise him.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:46.280
His hair has turned completely white and his ordeal had driven him stark, staring mad.

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:52.199
The tragedy changed the lighthouse world forever.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:35:08.040
From then, until the mid-1990s, when they were all fully automated, every lighthouse in the UK was assigned a three-man crew so that if disaster should overtake one of them, there would still be two to help keep each other sane.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:23.160
The coast of southern Wales is an ever-changing wonder.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:23.680
Its shores have shifted over millennia.

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:35.520
The drifting patterns of sand and tide conceal and reveal clues to those who once lived here.

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:44.040
On my journey around Gower I've landed at Port Eynon, once a swampy marshland.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:50.360
I've come to this landscape to search out a 6,000-year-old secret for our guide.

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:59.400
Tread lightly when walking on this beach at Port Eynon, because you're walking in the footsteps of our ancient ancestors.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:09.720
Archaeologist Rhiannon Philp is part of a Cardiff University team that has made a rare discovery on this stretch of Gower's coast.

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:15.720
Human footprints preserved in prehistoric peat.

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:17.920
How on earth did you find these footprints among all the contemporary ones?

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:22.800
So, most of the contemporary ones are within the sand, whereas what we are looking for is footprints in the peat.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:29.280
So, on this beach there's quite a lot of peat exposed at low tide.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:39.160
So, these are ancient footprints only available to the naked eye at very low tide, which is why we can't see them at this time of year?

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:42.040
Have you got any examples?

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:42.440
I have.

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:42.680
So this is an adult footprint.

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:48.400
It's quite eroded, but within it you can actually see their toe marks.

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:49.840
So, how frequently are footprints like this discovered?

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:51.760
They are actually quite rare in Britain.

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:57.680
There's probably only about nine or ten sites that have been recorded as having human footprints on the beaches.

00:36:56.920 --> 00:37:00.760
And three are right here on the South Wales coast.

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:02.560
How exciting.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:04.720
Is photography the best way of recording these?

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:08.080
It's one way, but we've also been experimenting with photogrammetry.

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:12.560
We've taken lots and lots of photographs all the way around, made a 3-D model and then we've been able to 3-D print that.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:14.480
And what's this showing us?

00:37:13.520 --> 00:37:19.200
So, this is probably a six-year-old child.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:21.400
We've got the big toe here.

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:23.760
It looks like he or she has got a very low instep.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:24.000
What's going on here?

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:27.840
This is a hoof print, basically.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:36.160
In other words, a child has trodden on the ground, and then superimposed on top of that is the print of a cloven-hoofed animal.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:39.080
Which suggests a slightly more advanced period, doesn't it?

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:47.160
Yeah, yeah, and suggests that they are utilising this kind of marshy environment for their livestock, which is quite interesting.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:54.320
I love the idea of a child running through the peat, herding its livestock.

00:37:53.520 --> 00:38:02.000
Our beaches are incredible time machines in many different ways.

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:13.240
Over the years on Coast, we've found the weird and wonderful buried beneath the sand.

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:20.640
But the next discovery going into our Great Guide is one unearthed at Pendine.

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:24.160
Seven miles of golden sand.

00:38:23.400 --> 00:38:28.760
But beach-goers here may be blissfully unaware of the sand's special qualities.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:36.280
It's superfine, which makes an exceptionally hard, flat surface.

00:38:35.320 --> 00:38:40.560
A rare combination, tailor-made for speed.

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:45.280
Neil paid a visit to Pendine to witness a remarkable resurrection.

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:52.480
In April 1926, this car was brought to this beach, and together they created history.

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:58.120
In the '20s was this really what was capable of the world record?

00:38:57.160 --> 00:39:00.320
This is a 171-mph land-speed-record-holding car.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:02.560
And this is still capable of high speeds?

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:05.760
It's certainly capable of doing the wrong side of 150mph.

00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:16.320
But given that it's a World War I aircraft engine, it takes a little persuasion to get started.

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:22.560
When it does get going, it's something to behold.

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:33.240
In the '20s, this beach was the only place in Britain big enough and flat enough to really let Babs rip.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:50.000
Babs was the car owned by Wales' very own magnificent man in a flying machine, Parry-Thomas.

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:57.520
He vied with Pendine's other famous racer, Scotsman Malcolm Campbell, to be the fastest man on earth.

00:39:56.600 --> 00:40:00.840
In March 1927, it was Thomas' turn to try to regain the crown.

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:16.440
As he slowed down beyond the measured mile, there was a cloud of spray and sand and it had all gone wrong, and the car had had a major accident at a significant speed, and Thomas was killed.

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:27.640
Once it lost control, Parry-Thomas never stood a chance.

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:32.200
Hard sand and high speed are an unforgiving combination.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:42.760
In an almost pagan ritual, Babs' seats were slashed and her dial smashed before she was buried in the dunes.

00:40:41.920 --> 00:40:53.480
In 1968, Owen Wyn Owen excavated the wreckage and painstakingly brought Babs back to life.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:41:02.840
Now, occasionally he brings the car back to the beach that Babs and Parry-Thomas once made the fastest place on earth.

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:18.640
Babs was back on the beach this year for her 90th anniversary, driven by Geraint Owen, the son of the man who painstakingly restored her.

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:28.800
This is the Great Coast Guide to southern Wales.

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:35.920
My journey around Gower has taken me from Mumbles to Oxwich Bay.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:42.400
I'm headed for Penclawdd, but to get there I need to negotiate Worm's Head.

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:52.560
As the most dramatic natural landmark on this peninsula, it just had to go in our Great Guide.

00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:55.600
I'm braving this infamous serpent by sea.

00:41:55.880 --> 00:42:02.440
But if you dare to venture out on foot, it pays to check your tide times.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:05.240
Once again, Neil was the right man for the job.

00:42:05.360 --> 00:42:11.160
The scramble across the jagged causeway that connects it to the mainland isn't for the faint-hearted.

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:11.680
I've got to read the tides right.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:21.760
The current that comes swirling in across the rocks can easily cut you off or wash you away.

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:24.560
You can't afford to hang around.

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:30.680
One adventurer who got himself marooned out here was the poet Dylan Thomas.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:37.440
He told tales of being trapped on the rocks by the rising tide as darkness fell.

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:47.480
Once you've scrambled along the rocks of the low neck, you reach a jagged arch cut by the sea clean through the body of the beast.

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:49.720
It takes you to the outer head, the loneliest tip of Gower.

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:57.520
They call this the Devil's Bridge, and I'd love to cross over and carry on, but I'm going to have to leave that little slice of heaven to the birds.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:09.600
I'm here in May and at this time of year, the tip of Worm's Head is strictly out of bounds because the sea birds are busy nesting.

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:15.800
This isolated spit of land offers sanctuary for wildlife.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:22.240
Today, I'm going in search of the marine life that's drawn to Worm's Head, with an expert.

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:25.240
This is Andrew Price's local patch.

00:43:24.280 --> 00:43:33.480
"Worm" actually derives from the Viking word "wyrm", which means "serpent" or "dragon".

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:35.480
So when you look at it from the sea you can really understand why they would have thought that.

00:43:35.320 --> 00:43:37.880
I've been really struck by how dramatic the landscape is.

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:41.920
That limestone rock, it's like it's been literally carved by Vikings.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:44.040
It's been carved, but not by Vikings.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:48.000
It's been carved by erosion from the sea, really, and that's what gives us this distinctive landmass.

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:49.280
It's desolate.

00:43:50.440 --> 00:43:57.920
I can see now why it's this area of protected beauty.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:58.560
Andy, this is unbelievable.

00:43:57.680 --> 00:43:59.280
Look what's straight in front of us!

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:03.400
Well, we've got Atlantic grey seals on the rocks, mostly females.

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:05.320
They've got the light underbelly and mottled fur.

00:44:06.640 --> 00:44:08.520
I just can't believe how chilled out they are.

00:44:07.640 --> 00:44:09.800
We are this close and they haven't moved.

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:10.440
This is probably as close as we should really get to them.

00:44:09.480 --> 00:44:14.400
If we'd come down from the rocks, would it have been a different case?

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:18.000
Would they have got spooked more easily?

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:20.480
Coming from the land, they would see us as a land-based predator.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:26.320
Because we are in kayaks, we're just seen as another seagoing mammal, I guess.

00:44:27.640 --> 00:44:29.600
And there's a lot of them.

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:31.760
I see dozens every time I come round this north side of the Worm's Head.

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:37.600
Of course we've got a lot of herring gulls, and a nesting site towards the tip there.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:42.280
We also have guillemots and razorbills in pretty much the same area as well.

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:46.800
So it's a huge nature reserve, massive bird colonies all over the Worm's Head.

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:53.680
What's staggered me is so often you go on a safari or a wildlife trek and you see nothing.

00:44:52.720 --> 00:44:55.600
But today it's been like a sort of feast of wildlife.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:57.200
A buffet of nature, isn't it?

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:59.040
Yeah, I was trying to think of the word, something like "elemental".

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:06.520
I'm discovering the very best the coast of southern Wales has to offer.

00:45:06.760 --> 00:45:14.000
Its headlands and islands give sustenance to some of Britain's richest wildlife.

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:21.000
But one island off the Pembrokeshire coast is a cut above the rest.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:23.960
A breeding colony for feathered friends.

00:45:23.760 --> 00:45:33.560
Skomer attracts around 10,000 puffins every year, and flies straight into our Guide.

00:45:32.720 --> 00:45:36.320
Miranda got wet to get close to them.

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:42.640
That is chilly, it's very chilly.

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:50.840
Puffins are easily spooked, so we have to be patient and move slowly.

00:45:50.360 --> 00:46:03.000
But we are being rewarded with a rare chance of swimming within just a few feet of them.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:12.960
Some of Skomer's grey seals are lounging nearby, but, for me, it's the puffins that steal the show.

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:18.520
Absolutely surrounded by puffins, maybe just five or six feet away from me.

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:20.680
Some of them just skimming over the top of my head.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:21.920
Incredible.

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:35.400
Seven miles from Skomer, Grassholm is one of the most important gannet colonies on earth.

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:43.560
Every spring, over 70,000 flock here from Africa and Spain.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:57.320
In breeding season, this tiny rock, barely half a mile across, is home to 7% of the world gannet population.

00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:08.720
On my voyage around the Gower Peninsula, I've reached my final destination, Penclawdd on the Burry Estuary.

00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:14.920
This tiny hamlet has a big reputation for a delicacy that's world-famous.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:21.760
A marine mollusc that lives beneath the mud.

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:24.360
How well do you know your Magna Carta?

00:47:23.480 --> 00:47:32.200
Because it was that agreement 800 years ago that ensured King John's subjects were given certain rights, such as access to a fair trial.

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:40.880
But less well-known is that it also enshrined the right for everybody to be allowed to pick 8lb of cockles from the foreshore.

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:46.560
And that right still exists in most parts of Britain today.

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:52.440
Pick more than 8lb of cockles, and you'll need a licence.

00:47:51.480 --> 00:48:00.360
For generations, the Jones family has had that license to harvest Wales' finest cockles from this foreshore.

00:47:59.840 --> 00:48:12.760
Cockles have been gathered on the Burry inlet for hundreds of years, by women at the beginning, walking out barefooted with their donkeys, gathering the catch, just the same way we do today with a rake and a riddle.

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:18.080
Bringing the cockles ashore, cooking them on the shore then selling them at local markets in Swansea.

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:21.160
I'm the fifth generation in the family to be gathering cockles.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:22.960
Have you got some pictures?

00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:23.440
I have.

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:23.840
So we've got the very early days.

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:26.680
There's a donkey which they used to carry the cockles on.

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:28.560
There's probably 50-60 kilos in that sack.

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:31.960
So one old woman picking 100 kilos of cockles in a day.

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:33.000
Yep.

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:33.480
My goodness!

00:48:32.520 --> 00:48:35.560
And here's my grandmother.

00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:39.760
She was gathering cockles up until a few years ago.

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:41.480
Look at that look.

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:42.680
They're sharing a moment, aren't they?

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:42.880
Yeah, there's nobody else to talk to!

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:43.200
Why was it women in the early days?

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:45.880
It's now predominantly male, I presume?

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:49.760
Yeah, most of the men worked in the mines, local mines, in and around this area.

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:53.600
The women went out to do the cockle gathering.

00:48:52.680 --> 00:49:01.960
It was seen as a woman's job, and I think my grandfather was one of the first men in the village to actually become a cockle gatherer at the age of about 11.

00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:05.000
And why particularly this estuary, this inlet?

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:09.080
We're so far south, I think, and we are on the Gulf Stream.

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:12.080
We think this is the best, sweetest cockle we can get around.

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:14.280
And that's the fleshy bit that you eat, is it?

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:14.640
That's the fleshy bit.

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:15.480
Lovely, it looks delicious.

00:49:14.520 --> 00:49:15.640
Taste that.

00:49:16.520 --> 00:49:17.600
Mm.

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:17.920
Nice?

00:49:17.120 --> 00:49:18.440
It is good, actually.

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:19.960
It's kind of sweet, you're right.

00:49:20.080 --> 00:49:21.960
It is really sweet.

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:25.520
Almost a lemony something...

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:25.960
It tastes like it's been seasoned.

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:27.360
Yes, it's got a taste of its own.

00:49:26.680 --> 00:49:27.920
Yeah, it really does.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:32.360
That's the first time I've had a raw cockle.

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:33.440
That's delicious.

00:49:32.680 --> 00:49:34.520
Really delicious.

00:49:34.840 --> 00:49:39.160
And is it a skill, cockling?

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:39.640
Somebody like me...

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:42.040
It's a way of life, it's a way of life.

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:42.800
It's like everything.

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:54.800
A miner would go and dig coal, we go and dig cockles, and we just love it because we are out in the open air, and we just love our job.

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:57.160
So, could you take me now and show me how to cockle?

00:49:57.800 --> 00:50:02.480
I can show you how to cockle, whether you would cockle, I don't know.

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:04.240
But it's worth having a go at it.

00:50:06.080 --> 00:50:10.400
I'll be looking to learn the craft of cockling later.

00:50:09.640 --> 00:50:15.560
But it's great to see the Joneses, father and son, still plying their trade here.

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:24.360
For other family businesses, changing times have brought generations of tradition to an end, as we found with the remarkable story of the Severn Princess.

00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:33.080
Beneath the shadow of the Severn Bridge is a jetty that once bustled with travellers.

00:50:32.240 --> 00:50:40.360
60 years ago, before the bridge, there was a ferry.

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:44.040
The Severn Princess sails into our guide as a genuine piece of estuary heritage.

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:50.800
Seven years ago, we visited her at her new home in Chepstow.

00:50:50.800 --> 00:50:54.800
My name is Richard Jones.

00:50:54.000 --> 00:51:04.680
I'm the oldest grandson of Enoch Williams, who was the founder of the last incarnation of the Beachley Aust Ferry.

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:08.800
This boat on which we are standing at the moment is the Severn Princess.

00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:14.760
This crossing was very important because it was the only crossing available for car traffic.

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:17.240
It was a lifeline to many people in their daily business.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:18.400
Many people courted on the ferries.

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:23.680
Girls in England meeting gentleman from Wales, and vice versa.

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:31.280
The last day that the service carried cars was September 8th, 1966, the day that the Severn Bridge opened.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:38.040
To commemorate the first crossing of the Severn Bridge, I have great pleasure in unveiling this plaque.

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:43.160
It was a joyous day in some ways, because everybody likes a party.

00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:50.000
But it was also very sad to see my grandfather's lifelong work come to an end.

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:57.600
Today, the ferry refuses to sink without trace.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:52:07.160
Richard is now a key member of the Severn Princess Restoration Group, working tirelessly to preserve the Princess.

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:14.000
Most of the work now has been trying to stop the weather getting at her in terms of rust.

00:52:13.040 --> 00:52:20.280
But actually, the structure is pretty sound, considering what the boat has been through in the 50 years since the ferry finished running.

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:27.440
She still is very vulnerable but, happily, we've been able to slow down that deterioration.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:33.520
I just feel very much attached to her and I would like to see her preserved.

00:52:35.080 --> 00:52:44.800
The group hope that the Princess will one day sit proud in a Heritage Park on the Wales coastal path.

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:52.240
On the coast of Gower, history is very much alive and kicking.

00:52:51.280 --> 00:53:00.840
I'm about to be schooled in a cockle gathering technique practised here in Penclawdd for centuries.

00:52:59.960 --> 00:53:02.760
You can see the cockles are just underneath here.

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:04.120
And as I'm scraping them back...

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:04.560
Yeah.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:05.960
There's cockles here, see?

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:11.560
You can feel them under your rake, can't you?

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:14.360
Yes.

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:17.800
I'm getting competitive.

00:53:17.200 --> 00:53:18.320
I don't want you to get more than me.

00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:20.200
Forget your 50 years' experience!

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:23.240
Not bad, is it?

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:28.480
You've got way more than me!

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:29.400
Why do they live just under the sand?

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:40.320
When the tide comes in, the peaks of the cockles will come up, and they feed, filtering the water.

00:53:39.360 --> 00:53:41.240
There's millions of cockles, and they all squirt up some water at the same time.

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:48.960
And it's where we say the cockles sing.

00:53:48.080 --> 00:53:52.480
I'm not feeling the urge to burst into song, myself.

00:53:51.920 --> 00:54:02.760
After half an hour of cockle picking, it's hard to believe Brian's mother spent hours doing this well into her 70s.

00:54:01.800 --> 00:54:05.080
It's actually backbreaking.

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:13.400
Cockles have fed the people of the Burry Estuary for hundreds of years.

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:14.200
But far older sea creatures can be found on these shores.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:23.600
In Victorian times, the coast of southern Wales attracted scientists searching for evidence to challenge the Church's view of creation.

00:54:22.760 --> 00:54:33.040
In a secluded cove, JW Salter unearthed a find so special it rewrote our understanding of the world.

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:36.800
Our Great Guide salutes the man and his discovery.

00:54:35.840 --> 00:54:39.960
Hermione Cockburn followed in his footsteps.

00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:49.120
In 1862, Salter's boat took a wrong turning, and he landed, purely by chance, at this rocky inlet near St Davids called Porth y Rhaw.

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:59.200
Salter uncovered evidence here that supported the idea that the Earth hadn't just existed for thousands of years, it had to be hundreds of millions of years old.

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:05.760
A literal reading of the Bible suggested the world was around 6,000 years old.

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:09.880
Salter found a fossil that said otherwise.

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:11.400
Hi, Bob.

00:55:10.560 --> 00:55:18.880
Dr Robert Owens knows that priceless fossil better than most.

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:21.600
So, Bob, tell us about what Salter found here.

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:21.920
Well, he found these.

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:23.040
My goodness!

00:55:22.160 --> 00:55:25.920
Giant trilobites.

00:55:25.120 --> 00:55:28.000
This one I'm holding in my hands comes from this very spot.

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:29.040
This is enormous.

00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:33.040
Imagine splitting a rock open and that's facing you.

00:55:32.080 --> 00:55:35.800
What would this creature have been like when it was living?

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:42.520
Well, it's a distant relative of the crabs, lobsters, scorpions, spiders, the arthropods, in other words.

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:43.120
That group of animals.

00:55:42.280 --> 00:55:49.800
This probably lived on the seabed, crawling around, and it was probably a predator-scavenger.

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:51.760
It was probably fairly high up in the food chain.

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:53.040
How old are these trilobites?

00:55:52.320 --> 00:55:59.080
On our present estimates, they are about 505 million years old.

00:55:58.360 --> 00:56:00.520
505!

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:01.080
So, a lot, lot older than any dinosaur, for example.

00:56:00.120 --> 00:56:05.920
Yes, I mean, over twice as old as the oldest dinosaur.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:07.280
Really right back to the beginnings of large life forms.

00:56:06.440 --> 00:56:08.800
That's right.

00:56:08.840 --> 00:56:13.320
And this geological period that they come from, it's called the Cambrian.

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:13.800
After...

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:16.040
After Wales, where rocks of this age were first recognised.

00:56:15.640 --> 00:56:17.320
A truly Welsh fossil, then.

00:56:16.640 --> 00:56:19.800
If there were to be a national fossil of Wales, I think this might well be it.

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:29.520
My own expedition to the Burry inlet has turned up a bucket full of specimens.

00:56:28.960 --> 00:56:31.080
We've got so many, they are spilling over.

00:56:31.880 --> 00:56:33.040
You can carry that.

00:56:32.120 --> 00:56:34.280
I knew you were going to say that.

00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:34.840
I'll carry that one.

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:35.120
Ho-ho!

00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:37.680
It weighs a tonne!

00:56:36.960 --> 00:56:38.840
It does.

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:39.760
There we go.

00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:42.240
Let's see if we can make something out of this sand.

00:56:41.400 --> 00:56:43.560
So what do we do now with them?

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:44.520
We are going to try to wash them, now.

00:56:43.560 --> 00:56:44.840
OK.

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:45.360
Where do we wash them?

00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:48.040
A drop of water, they'll be fine.

00:56:50.080 --> 00:56:51.120
If you put your sieve down there.

00:56:51.040 --> 00:56:52.840
OK.

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:54.880
And put yours flat.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:58.560
Are you just tipping, to get the excess sand off?

00:56:57.600 --> 00:56:58.720
Yeah, we're going to take that sand off there.

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:04.920
Very rewarding to get cockles from a piece of sand.

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:06.160
And people will take them home, and make some money on them.

00:57:07.720 --> 00:57:12.400
It's like something for nothing, although it isn't, because it's hard work.

00:57:11.960 --> 00:57:13.120
Yes.

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:13.720
Perfect job.

00:57:12.760 --> 00:57:14.560
Good stuff.

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:20.880
And how long would you expect to take to fill a sack?

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:21.240
Not very long.

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:29.680
It would take me at least 4-5 hours to make at least 10 of them bags filled to the top.

00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:32.360
And you're tired by then - tell me you're tired!

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:36.480
You want to go home then, you want to lie down somewhere after that.

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:43.200
I want to lie down right now to be honest with you!

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:44.400
And I've only got half a bag!

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:53.280
At the end of my journey, I'm exhausted... ..but looking forward to tasting some of the natural bounty of Gower.

00:57:54.520 --> 00:58:08.520
A peninsula bursting with wildlife and breathtaking views, on a coastline stuffed with stories.

00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:29.760
Touring with our Great Guide, at Freshwater West we surfed wind and waves... ..on Gower's epic edge, we explored a landmark like no other... ..at Pendine Sands, we walked in the wake of Britain's fastest men... ..and went to sea in search of wildlife.

00:58:28.800 --> 00:58:46.320
This is a coastline built by natural beauty, natural resources, and an industrious people who make the most of what surrounds them.

00:58:45.400 --> 00:58:50.760
A treat for the eyes and the taste buds.

00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:57.200
There's so much packed into this amazing stretch of Welsh coast, that really wherever you go, you're bound to find something new.
